---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../../components/PageHead.astro';
import Block from '../../components/Block.astro';
---
<Base
  title="How the Journeyletter works · Pezi Labs"
  description="What the Journeyletter is and the four rules it runs under."
  crumbs={[{ href: '/writing/', label: 'Journeyletter' }, { label: 'How it works' }]}
>
  <PageHead num="Writing" label="Journeyletter" title="How the Journeyletter works" />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:var(--s6)">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid block">
        <div class="c-label label mono"><b>00</b><span>About</span></div>
        <div class="copy">
  <p>
    The Journeyletter is field notes on operating AI agents for real work, written for
    serious agent-builders. It documents one practitioner's stack — the delegation
    policies, the contracts, the struggles, the reversals — as they actually happen. It
    is published by <a href="/">Pezi Labs</a> and edited by Tom Murphy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two lanes, same masthead. <a href="/notes/">Notes</a> is generated from exhaust, then
    edited — the four rules below describe it exactly. <a href="/letters/">Letters</a> is
    the opposite origin: Tom's own draft first, structured and edited from there. Both are
    real, both carry a colophon; they differ in where the first draft came from.
  </p>
          <p>The four rules, as they apply to Notes:</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Block num="01" label="Rule" title="Exhaust, not authorship" id="r1">
    <p>Nobody sits down to "write a post." The work happens; an agent harvests the exhaust —
    repo history, decision records, session transcripts — and surfaces when there's a
    piece worth making. A publication that demands homework from its author dies.</p>
  </Block>
  <Block num="02" label="Rule" title="Cadence by accumulation" id="r2">
    <p>It publishes when there's something real, not on a calendar. No filler, ever. If you
    don't hear from it, nothing worth your time happened.</p>
  </Block>
  <Block num="03" label="Rule" title="The wall" id="r3">
    <p>Patterns, struggles, and learnings cross into these pieces. Personal data never does
    — health, family, and finances stay categorically behind the wall, filtered out at
    the harvest stage before a draft exists. Field notes, not oversharing.</p>
  </Block>
  <Block num="04" label="Rule" title="Full transparency" id="colophons">
    <p>Every piece ends with a colophon naming the stack, the agents that did the work, and
    exactly what the human did. The colophons are structured data, and over time they are
    the experiment: watch the human's role shrink from author to editor to mentor, piece
    by piece, with receipts.</p>
  </Block>
</Base>
